==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|indirect|-ly|id2=adverbial}} or {{af|en|in-|directly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪndɪˈɹɛktli/|/ˌɪndaɪˈɹɛktli/}}
* {{hyph|en|in|di|rect|ly}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-indirectly.ogg|a=US}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# In an [[indirect]] manner.
#: {{ux|en|His alcoholism had a direct effect on his health, and '''indirectly''' caused problems for others.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|author=Roy Porter|title=Flesh in the Age of Reason|page=205|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2004
|passage=For while he refers to his gout (a good disease), he does not mention the complaint which '''indirectly''' killed him, his hydrocele – an enlargement of the scrotum.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|undirectly}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|directly}}

